<p>As the Iran conflict widens, Spain’s Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez again said Spain will not support the U.S.-Israeli military campaign against Iran and urged a diplomatic path instead—despite President Trump’s threat to cut off trade with Spain. Sánchez cast Madrid’s stance as a consistent “no to war” position, said Spain would not act out of fear of reprisals, and warned the strikes mark a dangerous escalation with wider consequences. At the heart of the standoff is Spain’s decision to deny U.S. forces access to the jointly used bases at Rota and Morón for missions tied to the strikes on Iran—something Washington says weakens allied support as the conflict spreads. EU officials and several European leaders signaled support for Spain, stressing that EU trade policy is handled collectively, which complicates any effort to single out Spain commercially. Even as the rhetoric sharpens, Europe is still looking for a coordinated response to the war’s security and economic fallout. Still, the EU’s swift public show of solidarity with a member state points to a continued push to keep transatlantic ties stable and rules-based, even under strain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/0MioPU6t0B9SvEqooD2w9sUs_N9y_Al102SF_uenBsYH-MgKKa53E5DaR5cUvWMGWjLvcNqFBMV14SukzlsimSPZrg1IdQNwf1J_1DHOcmjNCBTBgSzR9CUhuBcxsK9u9reThAnklZ6Zp44afbybonwEB3ypmjEvvb5uLYqQtCXb6v0N0ur7INQhzeFfDkxXDAn03Qb3EyrWiO40WzH7QkXG5rKvI69NQSwuJQbwyC9iTHsaS7DvNqX5JQ-3Z6uma6CZdB8V71J5edHN9Q' alt='Spain&#x27;s Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez delivers a televised statement on March 4, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Escalation warning: Sánchez likened the expanding conflict to “playing Russian roulette with the destiny of millions,” saying the strikes could trigger broader, unpredictable consequences.</li><li>Brussels political pushback: European Commission Vice President Teresa Ribera questioned whether Washington could follow through and urged the EU to meet Trump’s “bully tone” with unity.</li><li>NATO linkage: Coverage tied Trump’s criticism of Madrid not only to the Iran operation but also to Spain’s pushback against higher NATO defense-spending targets, a point that surfaced during his meeting with Germany’s chancellor.</li></ul><blockquote>We say no to war. - Pedro Sánchez</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Government of Spain (Pedro Sánchez): Sánchez said Spain would not be “complicit” in the Iran war and called it “naive” to think violence solves conflicts, invoking Spain’s 2003-era “no to war” tradition. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-04/pedro-sanchez-on-the-us-and-israeli-attack-on-iran-no-to-war-we-will-not-support-this-disaster.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>United States (President Trump): Trump said the U.S. would “cut off all trade with Spain” after Madrid refused base access linked to the Iran strikes. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/03/world/middleeast/trump-spain-trade.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>European Commission: A Commission spokesperson said the EU is in “full solidarity” with member states and is “ready to act if necessary” to safeguard EU interests. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978305/eu-expresses-solidarity-with-spain-following-trumps-trade-threat-madrid-says-it-wont-be-vassals'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>France (President Emmanuel Macron): Macron’s office said he expressed “solidarity” with Sánchez after the U.S. trade threat. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/04/eu-says-ready-to-defend-interests-after-trump-threatens-spain_6751087_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Germany (Chancellor Friedrich Merz): In an Oval Office appearance, Merz did not publicly object as Trump rebuked Spain. He later stressed the need to persuade Spain to meet shared security commitments in NATO terms, according to coverage of the meeting. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/donald-trump-berates-spain-friedrich-merz-listens/a-76214453'>DW</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera framing: Al Jazeera highli